I've been playing PL9 for about six months and I must extend compliments
to the development team. It's quite a big improvement over 2.3!

 I do have some reservations about polypiling though. This technique is
so powerful that it seems to unbalance the game. I find my own games
dividing into pre-wand of polymorph and post-w.o.p stages. I admit that
polypiling is a lot of fun, but for me it has a brute force quality that
seems to lack style. I rarely get nice armor (shield of reflection, cloak
of displacement etc.) or enough potions of gain level or gain ability by
any other method. But to simply eliminate it would be a very big strategic
loss for a player. My basic suggestion is that the game would benefit from the
removal of randomly generated w.o.p.s and a some compensatory additions.

Some ideas about alternatives (perhaps 2 and 3 are mutually exclusive):

1. w.o.p. on castle level.
How about replacing the wand of wishing on the castle level with a wand
of polymorph? If I find a wand of polymorph I rarely need wishes. The
game is still playable without a w.o.p. by that stage (but more challenging!)
At the same time make the generation of nice armor and potions more frequent
(let Mordor orcs have the odd pair of gauntlets of dexterity). Alternatively
one could, for example, let captains wear gauntlets of dexterity, master
lichs have helms of brilliance, lichs or other high level monsters have
cloaks of displacement etc. This would give some incentive for players to keep
lichs, say, in the game (I usually wipe them all out with a blessed scroll of
genocide before I even encounter one).

2. special ordering in shops.
What about allowing characters to place special orders in a shop? This
would be similar to wishing, the (crucial) difference though would be
that one would not get the item at once (and without enchantments?)
For example, an order for a pair of jumping boots might be ready after the
character has gained four additional experience levels (depending on the item
ordered). Only one extant order should be allowed (i.e. one item per order, no
additional orders until the ordered item arrives). One might want to limit
this to, say, general stores only (but it may then be necessary to ensure
that exactly one general store is generated per game). Of course the price
should probably be greatly increased but this would give characters
something to do with the gold pieces that are so prevalent in the game (but
often have so little use). It would also eliminate a lot of the randomness
that makes comparing game scores so difficult. Similarly it would help
to remove what for me is, frankly, tedium in waiting for nice items to be
randomly generated.

3. new tools.
How about a skinning kit (sewing kit?)? I like very much that a player
can make dragon scale mail from a dragon corpse. What about extending
this? Examples - a leacrotta corpse could be made into boots of speed,
a lich (orc shaman?) corpse could be made into a cloak of magic resistance
etc. The current method for making dragon scale mail would also be
superceded by this tool.

Someone suggested before that a method to make potions analogeous to the
technique for making scrolls of choice should be added. I second this
idea, perhaps the best way would be to add another tool - a chemistry
(alchemy) set.

Another suggestion I would like to second is the addition of sandpaper to
make a shield of reflection.

Another addition over 2.3 that I really like is the addition of intermediate
goals (castle, tower etc.). What about adding small, progressively harder,
strongholds that have the suggested tools or some of the nicer current
ones (one per stronghold). These could be added after level 15 or so.

Comments?

